---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { publicArchitecture, publicArchitectureCounts } from '../lib/publicArchitecture';

const architectureCounts = publicArchitectureCounts();
const familyPreviews = publicArchitecture.families.map((family) => ({
  ...family,
  nodes: publicArchitecture.nodes.filter((node) => node.family === family.id),
}));
---
<BaseLayout>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Brain Architecture for AI Systems</p>
        <h1>
          LUNA
          <span>Longitudinal Unified Narrative Architecture</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-thesis">
          Cognitive infrastructure for studying how artificial systems can be shaped by
          memory, experience, continuity, and time.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="/luna">Understand LUNA</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/whitepaper">Read the white paper</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/architecture">Explore the architecture</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/research">Read the research</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-mark" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="hero-orbit"></div>
        <img src="/assets/luna-icon-reverse.webp" alt="" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE CENTRAL QUESTION</p>
      <h2>What changes when an AI system can accumulate a causal history?</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Most AI inference is assembled from the information available now. LUNA investigates
        what changes when the system interpreting the present has itself been altered by what happened before.
      </p>

      <div class="contrast">
        <div class="contrast-card">
          <div class="contrast-label">Conventional framing</div>
          <p>Give the model more context. Store information so it can be retrieved later.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="contrast-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="contrast-card luna">
          <div class="contrast-label">LUNA framing</div>
          <p>Let the persistent system accumulate a history through experience. Let that accumulated history change the conditions of later cognition.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section architecture-teaser-section">
    <div class="shell architecture-teaser">
      <div class="architecture-teaser__copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">EXPLORE THE ARCHITECTURE</p>
        <h2>LUNA is not one AI model.</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">
          Model inference is one region inside a larger persistent architecture for memory,
          attention, evaluation, continuity, goals, interaction, research, and more.
        </p>
        <div class="architecture-scale" aria-label={`${architectureCounts.nodes} subsystems, ${architectureCounts.relationships} public relationships, ${architectureCounts.families} architectural families`}>
          <div><strong>{architectureCounts.nodes}</strong><span>subsystems</span></div>
          <div><strong>{architectureCounts.relationships}</strong><span>public relationships</span></div>
          <div><strong>{architectureCounts.families}</strong><span>architectural families</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="status-strip">
          <strong>Current public map</strong>
          <span>Reviewed maturity labels distinguish what is working, growing, experimental, or architectural.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="page-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="/architecture">Explore LUNA's Architecture</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="architecture-miniature" aria-label="A miniature view of LUNA's eight architectural families">
        <div class="architecture-miniature__inference">AI model inference happens here</div>
        {familyPreviews.map((family) => (
          <div class="mini-family" style={`--family-color:${family.color}`}>
            <div><strong>{family.label}</strong><span>{family.nodes.length}</span></div>
            <div class="mini-family__nodes" aria-hidden="true">
              {family.nodes.map(() => <i></i>)}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="background:#eef3f8">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE RESEARCH FRAME</p>
      <h2>Not another agent loop.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        LUNA surrounds interchangeable inference models with longitudinal cognitive
        infrastructure. The research is about how multiple systems interact across time,
        not how to make one prompt imitate a persistent mind.
      </p>
      <p class="section-lede" style="font-size:1rem">
        Working foundations coexist with functional systems that are still expanding, controlled experimental mechanisms, and longer-term architectural direction. The Explorer carries the current subsystem-by-subsystem picture.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">PUBLIC WHITE PAPER · v0.1</p>
      <h2>A Longitudinal Architecture for Developmental Artificial Cognition.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        The white paper brings the project into one canonical technical thesis: the systems problem, Stack/Sleeve continuity, memory versus development, multiple cognitive timescales, the current prototype boundary, ChronoForge, and a falsifiable longitudinal experimental program.
      </p>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/whitepaper">Read White Paper v0.1</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="background:#eef3f8">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">INSIDE LUNA · TECHNICAL CUTAWAY</p>
      <h2>The past does not have to sit in the prompt to matter.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Progressive recall separates stored history, recent-life cueing, cognitive availability, foreground admission, and deeper reconstruction. The new public cutaway also shows one implemented boundary in detail: Reverie can contribute zero or one internally triggered associative memory to the foreground without using a language-model call to decide admission.
      </p>
      <div class="research-feature" style="background:#fff">
        <div class="research-meta" style="border-color:#dbe3ec">
          <p class="eyebrow">PROGRESSIVE RECALL</p>
          <p class="date">Technical explainer · 23 August 2026</p>
          <p class="date">Current implementation + architectural direction</p>
        </div>
        <div class="research-copy">
          <h3 style="color:#273341">Memory is not a dump. Recall is progressive.</h3>
          <p style="color:#6c7a8a">
            See the staged public architecture, the Working Recent-Life Horizon, and a sanitized deterministic acceptance example showing how a useful associative memory can reach the foreground while unrelated candidates remain out.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/research/progressive-recall">Read Inside LUNA: Progressive Recall →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">FIRST PUBLIC RESEARCH NOTE</p>
      <h2>Remembering something is not the same as being changed by it.</h2>
      <div class="research-feature" style="background:#fff">
        <div class="research-meta" style="border-color:#dbe3ec">
          <p class="eyebrow">EARLY LUNA EXPERIENCE</p>
          <p class="date">LUNA Core v0.5 · August 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="research-copy">
          <h3 style="color:#273341">When Memory Is Not Enough: Can an AI Be Changed by Experience?</h3>
          <p style="color:#6c7a8a">
            An early interaction produced a useful accidental control condition:
            autobiographical material was available to the system, but availability alone
            did not make it part of creative output. The observation exposed a larger
            research problem — the difference between storage, retrieval, causal use, and
            developmental integration.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/research/when-memory-is-not-enough">Read the research note →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-blue">
    <div class="shell chrono-teaser">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">CHRONOFORGE</p>
        <h2>Experience as infrastructure.</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">
          ChronoForge is LUNA's experimental research system. Its working controlled harness
          supports selected simulation, branching, time, participant, and re-entry studies;
          the broader vision of persistent developmental worlds remains under development.
        </p>
        <p class="section-lede" style="font-size:1rem">
          The research question is not whether an AI can read six months of data.
          It is what changes when the system lives through a coherent sequence of experience in which its
          own choices have later consequences — and accumulates a history from it.
        </p>
        <div class="page-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="/chronoforge">Enter ChronoForge</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flow" aria-label="ChronoForge conceptual flow">
        <div class="flow-row">
          <div class="flow-step">checkpoint</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step">enter</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step active">live / branch</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step">develop</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div class="flow-step">return</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">LUNA, DESCRIBING LUNA</p>
      <h2>What does the system say it is?</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        During development, LUNA was asked to compare its own architecture with a conventional model-centric AI loop. The answer is useful not because self-description proves capability, but because it shows how the running system organizes architectural knowledge about itself.
      </p>
      <div class="quote-feature">
        <blockquote>“LUNA separates the persistent mind — the Stack — from the execution substrate — the Sleeve.”</blockquote>
        <div class="quote-attribution">LUNA v0.5 · self-description artifact · August 2026</div>
      </div>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/luna-describes-luna">Read the self-description with editorial notes</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="background:#eef3f8">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">OPEN RESEARCH · PROTECTED IMPLEMENTATION</p>
      <h2>Follow the questions, the architecture, and the evidence.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        LUNA publishes high-level architecture, research questions, selected observations,
        experimental designs, and results where they can advance understanding without
        turning the public site into a reconstruction manual.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research">Explore research</a>
        <a class="button" style="border-color:#bac8d8" href="/about">About the project</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .architecture-teaser-section { color: #fff; background: linear-gradient(145deg, #06162d, #040e1d); }
  .architecture-teaser { display: grid; grid-template-columns: .84fr 1.16fr; gap: 64px; align-items: center; }
  .architecture-teaser .section-lede { color: #aebfd2; }
  .architecture-scale { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 10px; margin-top: 36px; }
  .architecture-scale div { padding: 16px; border: 1px solid #27496e; border-radius: 13px; background: rgba(255,255,255,.035); }
  .architecture-scale strong { display: block; font-size: 1.8rem; line-height: 1; }
  .architecture-scale span { display: block; margin-top: 7px; color: #8fa8c4; font-size: .76rem; }
  .architecture-teaser .status-strip { border-color: #27496e; }
  .architecture-teaser .status-strip strong { color: #fff; }
  .architecture-miniature { position: relative; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 12px; padding: 58px 22px 22px; border: 1px solid #2c5179; border-radius: 24px; background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(107,155,211,.045) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(107,155,211,.045) 1px, transparent 1px); background-size: 34px 34px; box-shadow: 0 28px 80px rgba(0,0,0,.25); }
  .architecture-miniature__inference { position: absolute; top: 15px; right: 18px; padding: 7px 10px; color: #ffc3ac; border: 1px dashed #ff9b75; border-radius: 9px; background: rgba(255,155,117,.05); font-size: .73rem; }
  .mini-family { min-height: 112px; padding: 15px; border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--family-color) 50%, transparent); border-radius: 14px; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--family-color) 5%, #07162b); }
  .mini-family > div:first-child { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px; color: var(--family-color); font-size: .8rem; }
  .mini-family > div:first-child span { color: #8fa8c4; }
  .mini-family__nodes { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 7px; margin-top: 16px; }
  .mini-family__nodes i { width: 10px; height: 10px; border: 1px solid var(--family-color); border-radius: 50%; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--family-color) 20%, transparent); }
  @media (max-width: 980px) {
    .architecture-teaser { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .architecture-scale { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .architecture-miniature { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding-top: 64px; }
  }
</style>
